course: dictionary
lesson: 8666
title: Signposts in order: secondly
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: Secondly is a linking word. It means «in the second place», so it tells the reader that this is the second reason, step or point. It usually comes after «firstly» or «first», as in «First, we must check the map; secondly, we must pack the water.»
explain: Writers use words like this to make an argument easy to follow. Without them, reasons can run together and the reader gets lost. Secondly is an adverb, and it is usually followed by a comma.
explain: Look for the signposts. First or firstly shows the start, secondly shows the next point, and thirdly and finally come after that. When you read an explanation, follow the signposts to see how the writer has built the reasons.
fact: Secondly means «in the second place».
fact: Secondly gives the second point in a list of reasons or steps.
fact: It often comes after «firstly» or «first», as in «Firstly, ...; secondly, ...».
fact: Other signposts in the same family are thirdly and finally.
fact: Secondly helps a reader or listener follow an argument step by step.
exercise: What does secondly mean? | *In the second place | In a loud voice | In the first place | At the very end of the story
exercise: In maths, «Firstly, add the tens; secondly, add the ones.» Which step comes second? | *Add the ones | Add the tens | Multiply everything together
exercise: The writer has said «First, I was tired.» Which word should start the next reason? | Once upon a time | *Secondly | Yesterday | Firstly
test: Which word signals the second point after «Firstly, we should recycle»? | Lastly | Maybe | Dear | *Secondly
test: Why do writers use secondly? | *To show the reader the order of the points | To end a story with a surprise | To make the reader lose track of the order | To make a word longer
exam: Copy the signpost that shows the second point in: «First, pack a torch; secondly, pack a map; finally, pack a snack.» | pack | *secondly | finally | firstly
